  2700	> ```latex
  2701	> \paragraph{Relay.}  The run premise is the receipt premise of~\cite{DC}.
  2702	> An honest node forwards every block that it accepts and every vote and
  2703	> row that it accepts, also when it accepts the object from a block
  2704	> (\cite{DC}, lines 203 and 530).  When an honest node accepts an object
  2705	> at time~$t$, every honest node that does not hold it calls the handler
  2706	> of that object before $\max\{t,t_{\rm GST}\}+\Delta$, with its
  2707	> dependencies present.  This is DC's \textsf{PartialSynchrony.relay}
  2708	> (\textsf{handlesAt}).  The handler can refuse the object; for example,
  2709	> the vote pool refuses a third distinct vote of one seat.  The premise
  2710	> does not require acceptance.  One difference from~\cite{DC} is in the
  2711	> receiver exclusion: DC's receiver can skip a block that conflicts
  2712	> with~$F$; the raw SA tree of this document has no such exclusion,
  2713	> because the SA base processes every branch~(b).  The proofs derive the
  2714	> transport that they need from the handlers~\cite[\S3]{RR}: exact
  2715	> transport of block paths, in-window transport of a vote or of the
  2716	> equivocation of its seat, and exact transport of timely honest rows.
  2717	> At an honest proposal the reader's merge can differ from the proposer's
  2718	> vote set only in the votes of equivocating seats; the scores are
  2719	> equal~\cite[\S4.3]{RR}, as in DC's \textsf{ScorePreservingViewExtension}.
  2720	> ```
