  4680	- Statement (pinned):
  4681	
  4682	> _fj-redteam.md, lines 234–258:_
  4683	>
  4684	> F2 — PROVED. Suppose J is justified at j and a valid block W has
  4685	> h(W)>j with J not a prefix of W. On W's path, take the first crossing
  4686	> of j. It consumes q progress identities naming an entry T≠J at j.
  4687	> A target QC for T would conflict with J's target QC. Their intersection
  4688	> has at least 2q−n>a identities, so an honest identity would violate E2.
  4689	> Thus this crossing cannot be an all-target crossing. It needs the
  4690	> timeout branch, at the uniform maturity slot.
  4691	>
  4692	> More precisely, intersect J's target QC with the progress QC for T.
  4693	> At least one honest member of the intersection has already targeted J.
  4694	> Its row for T cannot be a target; it is a timeout. “Through timeouts”
  4695	> therefore means a mixed or all-timeout progress QC with at least one
  4696	> honest timeout. It does not mean that every progress row is a timeout.
  4697	>
  4698	> Now suppose a finality QC for J exists, before or after that crossing.
  4699	> Intersect it with the progress QC for T. Again an honest member must
  4700	> occur. If its progress row is a target for T, E1 forbids its finality
  4701	> pair for J. If it is a timeout, E1 also forbids that pair, regardless
  4702	> of the entry named by the timeout. This argument is independent of the
  4703	> order of emission. Therefore J has not finalized and cannot finalize
  4704	> in any continuation that retains the fault bound and honest client.
  4705	>
  4706	> This is the argument in [finalized_preceq_of_height_lt][crossing],
  4707	> with its direction reversed. It applies to a privately finalized J,
  4708	> and to a crossing held only by the adversary.
  4709	
